---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/surge-", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.5)
```

A force floods some areas with stop and search from a given month. Did
recorded crime fall? This vignette works through the question on a simulated
panel whose true answer is known, so that every estimate can be checked
against it.

```{r library}
library(streetlamp)
```

## A panel with a known answer

```{r simulate}
sim <- lamp_simulate(
  n_areas = 100, n_months = 30, design = "event",
  effect = -0.25, base_rate = 30, missing = 0.05, seed = 2026
)
truth <- attr(sim, "truth")
c(
  effect_on_affected_types = truth$effect,
  effect_on_crime_total = truth$effect_crime_total,
  treated_areas = length(truth$treated_areas)
)
```

The effect is applied to every crime type except bicycle theft, so the total
moves by less than `effect`: that smaller number is what an estimator on
`crime_total` should recover. Bicycle theft is left alone on purpose, to serve
as a placebo outcome later.

## Defining the treatment

```{r treatment}
tr <- lamp_treatment(
  sim, "event",
  date = truth$event_date, scope = truth$treated_areas, window = c(-12, 12)
)
tr
```

## Two-way fixed effects

```{r twfe}
fit <- lamp_twfe(sim, "crime_total", tr)
fit
```

The estimate is close to the truth, and the interval covers it. The print
method leads with the identifying assumption and with the number of
force-months dropped because the force did not submit, since both bear on
whether the number means anything.

```{r compare-truth}
c(estimate = fit$coefficients$estimate[1], truth = truth$effect_crime_total)
```

## The event study

A single number hides the path. The event study estimates one coefficient per
month relative to the intervention.

```{r event-study}
es <- lamp_event_study(sim, "crime_total", tr, window = c(-8, 8))
es
```

```{r plot-es, fig.alt = "Event study coefficients by month relative to the intervention, flat before and negative after"}
plot(es)
```

The pre-period coefficients are flat, which is the visible form of the
parallel-trends assumption.

## What the pre-trend test is worth

A pre-trend test that does not reject is weak evidence, and how weak is
computable.

```{r pretrends}
pt <- lamp_pretrends(es)
pt
```

The power table is the part that matters. It reports the linear trend the test
would catch half the time and four times in five, and the bias each would put
into the post-period estimates. If that bias is the same size as the estimate,
a passing test has told you very little.

## Placebo tests

Three ways of asking whether the specification manufactures effects.

```{r placebo-space}
lamp_placebo(fit, type = "space", n = 100, seed = 1)
```

```{r placebo-time}
lamp_placebo(fit, type = "time", n = 10)
```

```{r placebo-outcome}
lamp_placebo(fit, type = "outcome", outcome = "bicycle_theft")
```

Reassigning the treatment to random areas, or moving the date back into the
pre-period, produces nothing like the real estimate, and bicycle theft does not
move. Had any of them reproduced the result, the estimate would be measuring
something other than the intervention.

## Converting to crimes prevented

The question is usually asked in crimes, not log points. The conversion needs
to know how many searches the surge actually added.

```{r prevented}
lamp_crimes_prevented(fit, sim, stops_added = 8, per_stops = 1000, seed = 1)
```

The assumption chain is printed with the number because every link in it can
fail: the figure counts recorded crime only, applies an average effect at the
margin, and inherits the identifying assumption of the estimate it came from.

## When adoption is staggered

If areas start at different dates, this whole approach is wrong: relative-time
dummies in a two-way fixed effects regression use already-treated areas as
controls. The event study refuses rather than returning a biased number.

```{r staggered-refusal, error = TRUE}
staggered <- lamp_simulate(n_areas = 60, n_months = 30, design = "staggered", seed = 11)
ad <- attr(staggered, "truth")$adoption
tr_stag <- lamp_treatment(staggered, "staggered", adoption = ad[!is.na(ad$adoption_month), ])
lamp_event_study(staggered, "crime_total", tr_stag)
```

The staggered vignette continues from here.
